  <ExerciseCalculator activities={CYCLING} defaultIndex={1} />

    <Fragment slot="content">
    <ArticleImage src="/assets/images/articles/cycling-route.svg" alt="Illustration of a cyclist riding outdoors, tracking speed and calorie burn." />
    
    <h2>The Comprehensive Science of Cycling Calories Burned: Speed, Power, and Biomechanics</h2>
    <p>Cycling is widely recognized by exercise physiologists and sports scientists as one of the most metabolically demanding yet physically forgiving endurance activities available. Unlike running, which generates ground-reaction impact forces of 2.5 to 3.0 times your body weight with every stride, cycling is a closed-chain, non-weight-bearing movement. The bicycle frame supports your body weight, drastically reducing eccentric muscular damage and joint strain while allowing you to sustain elevated heart rates and massive caloric outputs for hours.</p>
    <p>However, calculating the exact calories burned while turning the pedals is notoriously complex. Your energy expenditure is not simply a function of time spent in the saddle; it is dictated by the interaction of rider body mass, rolling resistance, cadence, elevation gradient, and—above all—aerodynamic drag. Our cycling calories calculator harnesses the scientifically validated Metabolic Equivalent of Task (MET) framework from the Compendium of Physical Activities to provide precision estimates across all riding styles, speeds, and terrains.</p>

    <h2>The Biomechanics of the Pedal Stroke & Muscle Recruitment</h2>
    <p>A fluid cycling pedal stroke is a 360-degree kinetic chain that recruits nearly every major muscle group in the lower body, dividing the work across distinct phases of the crank rotation:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The Power Phase (12 o'clock to 5 o'clock):</strong> As you push down, your <em>gluteus maximus</em> and <em>quadriceps</em> fire aggressively to extend the hip and knee. This phase produces over 60% of total propulsion and consumes the bulk of metabolic energy.</li>
      <li><strong>The Bottom Transition (5 o'clock to 7 o'clock):</strong> The <em>calves (gastrocnemius and soleus)</em> contract to plantarflex the ankle, scraping the pedal backward as if scraping mud off your shoe.</li>
      <li><strong>The Upstroke & Recovery (7 o'clock to 12 o'clock):</strong> The <em>hamstrings</em> and <em>hip flexors (iliopsoas)</em> lift the pedal back toward the top of the stroke, stabilizing the crank and preparing for the next power phase.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>During low-to-moderate intensity endurance rides (Zone 2 cardio), your type-I slow-twitch muscle fibers dominate, utilizing aerobic beta-oxidation to burn stored adipose tissue (body fat) as primary fuel. As you increase speed or attack steep climbs, your body crosses the lactate threshold, recruiting type-II fast-twitch muscle fibers that burn muscle glycogen anaerobically, multiplying your caloric burn rate per minute.</p>

    <h2>Understanding METs in Cycling: How Speed Multiplies Calorie Burn</h2>
    <p>Our calculator categorizes cycling using validated MET values. One MET represents your resting metabolic rate (roughly 1 kcal per kilogram of body weight per hour). In cycling, energy cost does not scale linearly with speed; it scales exponentially due to fluid dynamics:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Casual Leisure Riding (&lt;10 mph / 16 km/h):</strong> 4.0 METs — Gentle recreational riding on flat bike paths with minimal cardiovascular strain.</li>
      <li><strong>Light Commuting (10–11.9 mph / 16–19 km/h):</strong> 6.0 METs — Steady pace with light pedaling effort; burns roughly 350–450 kcal/hr for an average adult.</li>
      <li><strong>Moderate Pace (12–13.9 mph / 19–22 km/h):</strong> 8.0 METs — Standard endurance pace; sustained aerobic conditioning.</li>
      <li><strong>Vigorous Training (14–15.9 mph / 22–25 km/h):</strong> 10.0 METs — Energetic group ride pace requiring active focus on breathing and cadence.</li>
      <li><strong>Fast Club / Race Pace (16–19 mph / 25–30 km/h):</strong> 12.0 METs — High cardiovascular demand; heavy reliance on glycogen.</li>
      <li><strong>Competitive Time-Trial / Racing (&gt;20 mph / &gt;32 km/h):</strong> Up to 15.8 METs — Elite-level output pushing near maximum aerobic capacity (VO2 max).</li>
    </ul>

