    <h2>The Physics of Aerodynamic Drag: Why Going Faster Costs So Much</h2>
    <p>On flat ground, once a cyclist exceeds 12 mph (19 km/h), aerodynamic resistance becomes the dominant resistive force, accounting for over 80% to 90% of total mechanical resistance. Aerodynamic drag force is proportional to the square of velocity, and the power required to overcome that drag is proportional to the cube of velocity.</p>
    <p>This means that increasing your riding speed from 12 mph to 18 mph does not require 50% more energy—it requires more than <strong>double the mechanical wattage</strong> from your legs. That exponential power demand translates directly into rapid carbohydrate oxidation and elevated caloric expenditure.</p>

    <h2>Indoor Spin Bikes vs. Outdoor Cycling</h2>
    <p>When riding a stationary bike indoors, several dynamic environmental factors disappear: there is zero headwind, no coasting down descents, and no road vibration. However, indoor cycling often produces comparable or even higher caloric burn rates for three reasons:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Zero Coasting:</strong> On a road bike, cyclists spend roughly 10% to 15% of their ride freewheeling or waiting at traffic intersections. On a stationary bike or smart trainer, pedaling is 100% continuous.</li>
      <li><strong>Thermoregulatory Heat Stress:</strong> Without natural ambient airflow to cool the skin, indoor riders sweat more profusely and experience higher cardiac drift, slightly increasing the energetic demand of circulation.</li>
      <li><strong>Structured HIIT Intervals:</strong> Spin classes frequently utilize high-intensity sprint intervals that induce Excess Post-Exercise Oxygen Consumption (EPOC), raising your post-workout resting burn.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Fueling and Recovery Guidelines for Cyclists</h2>
    <p>If you are cycling to create a <a href="/calorie-deficit-calculator/">calorie deficit</a> for fat loss, avoid the common mistake of severely under-fueling during the ride. For rides under 60 minutes, plain water and electrolytes are sufficient. For endurance rides exceeding 90 minutes, consuming 30 to 60 grams of fast-digesting carbohydrates per hour prevents fatigue and stops your body from catabolizing lean muscle tissue for fuel.</p>
    <p>To compare how cycling compares to other cardio modalities, explore our <a href="/running-calorie-calculator/">running calorie calculator</a>, <a href="/walking-calorie-calculator/">walking calorie calculator</a>, or see all activities in our general <a href="/calories-burned-calculator/">calories burned calculator</a>.</p>
    
